Description: reset-admin.sh: use this packaging's fixed FHS paths instead of
 detecting them from the script's own location, require the seafile identity,
 forward arguments to createsuperuser
 Upstream derives every path (data dir, central config, PYTHONPATH, RPC pipe,
 the seahub tree) from dirname(dirname($0)), assuming its own single mutable
 install tree; that never resolves correctly here, since binaries, config and
 data live in separate FHS locations (/usr, /etc, /var).
 Sources /etc/seafile/seafile.env instead, which already exports everything
 needed (PYTHONPATH, SEAHUB_DIR, SEAFILE_DATA_DIR, ...); keeps the one useful
 safety check (validate_seafile_data_dir) and the actual createsuperuser call,
 drops the rest of the path-detection/python-version-detection scaffolding as
 redundant with it. Re-execs as the seafile user via runuser when invoked as
 anyone else, before doing anything - the caller needs permission to become
 that user to run this at all, not just root's ability to bypass file checks.
 Also forwards arguments to createsuperuser (upstream's own wrapper dropped
 them too), so its --noinput/--email/--password flags become reachable for
 non-interactive use instead of only the interactive prompts.
Author: dionysius <dragon.dionysius@gmail.com>
Forwarded: not-needed
Comment: install-layout specific, not something upstream's own tar.gz/docker install needs.
